<section class="page-hero" aria-labelledby="rivers-title">
  <p class="eyebrow">Landscape Deep Dive</p>
  <h1 id="rivers-title">Rivers, habitat links and heritage</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Mukundara is a pair of long Vindhyan ridges held between rivers. The rivers mark its edges, the ridges and
    valleys connect it to other forests, and people have built forts, temples and lodges along both for
    centuries. This page explains all three, with the sources behind each statement.
  </p>
  <div class="badge-row" aria-label="Page status">
    <span class="badge">Sources checked October 2026</span>
    <span class="badge">No water-point or wildlife locations</span>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section split-panel" aria-labelledby="river-frame-heading">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">River Frame</p>
    <h2 id="river-frame-heading">Four rivers draw the outline</h2>
    <p>
      The Tiger Conservation Plan describes the drainage simply: the <strong>Chambal</strong> drains the reserve to the
      north-west, the <strong>Kalisindh</strong> to the east, the <strong>Ahu</strong> to the east and the
      <strong>Amjhar</strong> to the south. The <strong>Eru</strong> and <strong>Brahmani</strong> also draw on the
      same catchment. The two parallel ridges run for roughly 80 km from the Chambal to the Kalisindh and are only
      2 to 5 km wide, which is why the rivers sit so close to the forest edge.
    </p>
    <p>
      The plan also notes a key ecological fact: the perennial rivers mostly flow <em>along the outer boundary</em>.
      Inside, streams such as Junapani, Jhamra and Kadap ki Khal rise in the hills but are seasonal and stay dry for
      much of the year. That makes the dry months a pinch period for every animal that needs to drink.
    </p>
  </div>
