<section class="section" aria-labelledby="rivers-heading">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">River by River</p>
    <h2 id="rivers-heading">What each river does for the habitat</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="info-grid">
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">North-west edge</span>
      <h3>Chambal</h3>
      <p>
        The largest river of the landscape. A stretch from Garadia Mahadev to Jawahar Sagar dam, formerly part of the
        National Chambal Sanctuary, was included in the reserve's core in 2013. Gorges, deep pools and riverine
        cliffs support gharial, mugger, turtles, otters and fish-eating birds. A WII rapid assessment between Kota
        Barrage and Jawahar Sagar recorded 132 bird and 46 fish species along this connected stretch.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Eastern edge</span>
      <h3>Kalisindh</h3>
      <p>
        A 405 km Chambal tributary that rises in Dewas district, Madhya Pradesh, flows through Jhalawar and passes
        through the Darrah part of the reserve before joining the Chambal near Kharwan village in Kota district (WII).
        Sand mining and dry-season riverbed cultivation along it are named in the TCP as threats to wildlife and
        riverine habitat.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">East</span>
      <h3>Ahu</h3>
      <p>
        Flows north to meet the Kalisindh at Gagron near Jhalawar, the confluence that gives Gagron Fort its
        water-girt position. In the reserve it is one of the perennial rivers on the periphery.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">South</span>
      <h3>Amjhar</h3>
      <p>
        Drains the southern side of the hills. The TCP lists it among the perennial rivers on the periphery and,
        like the Kalisindh, names sand mining in its bed as a threat to habitat and animal movement.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Kota edge</span>
      <h3>Abheda</h3>
      <p>
        Abheda is not a river but a historic pond and palace on Kota's southern edge, listed as a historical site in
        the 2023 Zonal Master Plan and home to the Abheda Biological Park. It shows how town wetlands sit right
        against the reserve's northern buffer.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Inside the hills</span>
      <h3>Nalas, talabs and springs</h3>
      <p>
        Seasonal streams, small reservoirs (talab, talai) and a few perennial springs carry most wildlife through
        summer. This site deliberately does not list or map them, because dry-season water points concentrate
        animals and are the places poachers target.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p class="source-note">
    Sources: <a href="/resources/documents/mhtr-tiger-conservation-plan-bhainsrodgarh-core-addition/">Tiger Conservation Plan (updated, with Bhainsrodgarh)</a>,
    sections 2.1–2.2 and 3; <a href="/resources/documents/ntca-mukundara-brief-note/">NTCA brief note</a>;
    <a href="/resources/documents/researchgate-kota-barrage-jawahar-sagar-wildlife-status/">WII Kota Barrage–Jawahar Sagar assessment</a>;
    <a href="/resources/documents/wii-vibrant-ganga-kali-sindh-river/">WII Kali Sindh river profile</a>;
    <a href="/resources/documents/rajasthan-zmp-mhtr-2023/">ESZ Zonal Master Plan 2023</a>.
  </p>
</section>

