<section class="section split-panel" aria-labelledby="links-heading">
  <div>
    <p class="eyebrow">Habitat Connectivity</p>
    <h2 id="links-heading">Why the links between forests matter for every species</h2>
    <p>
      A reserve that is 80 km long and only a few kilometres wide has a very long edge. On its own it cannot hold
      large, healthy populations of wide-ranging animals, and small populations lose genetic variety over time. The
      TCP states that the adjoining landscape must act as a corridor “to ensure gene flow of the wildlife moving
      between the protected areas”.
    </p>
    <p>
      The NTCA brief note lists the links: Ramgarh Vishdhari and Ranthambhore to the north, Bhainsrodgarh to the
      south-west (now part of the core), and Gandhi Sagar Sanctuary in Madhya Pradesh to the south. It also notes
      that these links are fragmented by farms, roads and settlements and need restoring. River valleys, ravines,
      forest strips along nalas and even scrub hedges are what animals actually use. Leopards, sloth bears, wolves,
      hyenas, jackals, deer, birds and seed-carrying fruit-eaters all depend on them, and so do the plants whose
      seeds they spread.
    </p>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li><strong>Rivers as paths:</strong> the TCP records large-carnivore dispersal along the Chambal and Kalisindh valleys.</li>
      <li><strong>Roads and rail:</strong> highways and railway lines crossing the ridges need underpasses and speed control to stay permeable.</li>
      <li><strong>Edges matter:</strong> grazing, sand mining and land conversion at the edge decide whether a link stays usable.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <figure class="map-frame landscape-photo-frame landscape-natural-frame" style="--photo-ratio: 3 / 2;">
