<section class="section" aria-labelledby="heritage-heading">
  <div class="section-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">Cultural Heritage</p>
    <h2 id="heritage-heading">Forts, temples and old lodges along the ridges</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      The TCP lists important archaeological and religious sites “within as well as in the vicinity” of the
      reserve. They show that this has been a lived landscape for a very long time.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="info-grid">
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">UNESCO World Heritage</span>
      <h3>Gagron Fort, Jhalawar</h3>
      <p>
        A hill-and-water fort at the confluence of the Ahu and Kalisindh, surrounded by water on three sides. It is
        one of the six “Hill Forts of Rajasthan” inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2013.
      </p>
      <a href="https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/247" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UNESCO listing</a>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Chambal cliffs</span>
      <h3>Bhainsrorgarh fort</h3>
      <p>
        A fort town on a cliff above the Chambal at its meeting with the Bamani (also written Brahmani) river in
        Chittorgarh district. The Bhainsrodgarh Wildlife Sanctuary, added to the reserve's core in 2023, takes its
        name from this town.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Darrah hills</span>
      <h3>Abli Meeni Mahal and Rawatha Mahal</h3>
      <p>
        Old palaces and lodges inside or beside the reserve, listed in the TCP among its historical sites. The TCP
        notes that these forests were hunting reserves of the Kota, Bundi, Chittorgarh and Jhalawar princely states;
        old hunting towers (“malas”) still stand. Darrah became a sanctuary on 7 November 1955.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Near Rawatbhata</span>
      <h3>Baroli group of temples</h3>
      <p>
        A cluster of early-medieval (about 10th-century) temples on the Chambal near Rawatbhata, including the
        Ghateshwara temple, protected by the Archaeological Survey of India. The TCP lists it in the reserve's vicinity.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Living worship</span>
      <h3>Shrines in the forest</h3>
      <p>
        The TCP names Garadia Mahadev, Geparnath, Padajhar Mahadeo, Kalsiya Mahadeo, Ambarani Mataji and Nahar Singh
        Mataji among the religious sites in and near the reserve. Many sit at gorges, springs or cliff edges.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="info-card">
      <span class="route-kicker">Deep time</span>
      <h3>Temple ruins and rock paintings</h3>
      <p>
        The TCP also mentions temple remains and Stone Age rock paintings among the landscape's heritage. Rock art is
        fragile and easily damaged; this site does not publish its locations.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p class="source-note">
    Sources: <a href="/resources/documents/mhtr-tiger-conservation-plan-bhainsrodgarh-core-addition/">updated TCP</a> (historical and religious values; history of management);
    <a href="https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/247" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UNESCO World Heritage Centre, Hill Forts of Rajasthan</a>;
    Archaeological Survey of India (Jodhpur Circle) protected-monument listing for Baroli.
    Builders and dates of individual lodges in the Darrah hills are not stated in these sources and are not given here.
  </p>
</section>

